From 7d1e5a566b920f7ce45c9daa8cbc195efc407982 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: exeroom536 <352890649+exeroom536@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 23:23:34 +0500
Subject: [PATCH 51/54] super-amd: a second migration onto more disks, in place
 through AMD's log of the data

A volume whose data a migration onto more disks moved already sits at
AllocatedSpace + 0x800: the AMD driver moves it onto more disks again in
place (rcblob RC_SetupTransformParams: 0x110141), its regions as far as
the two layouts are apart, a stripe at a time where that is less than a
region, and below LogDataRange each stripe through a log of its data at
AllocatedSpace + 0x600 first, the phase in the transform log
(RC_MoveDataNextRegion, RC_TransformUpdateNextDynamicRegionFromBeginning).

mdadm --grow --raid-devices now records and runs such a migration of a
RAID0 or a RAID5 (md reshapes without a new data_offset, held a stripe
at a time, the stripe written to the log of the data before md moves
it), and assembly takes one stopped below LogDataRange - by the AMD
driver or by mdadm - there in user space before md runs the volume
(recover_backup_amd): md does not go on from below it.  Earlier in this
series such a volume stopped there was not assembled, or ran as it was,
its migration left recorded, if nothing had moved.  md is let on only
where the transform log says, what it moved flushed to the disks first.
Another chunk for such a volume is refused (the next patch): AMD would
move the data back up.

md held by sync_max at a point across a stripe of the old layout keeps
a read of that stripe waiting until it moves past it (raid5.c
stripe_ahead_of_reshape), and a write of suspend_lo/hi then waits in
mddev_suspend() for that read: mdadm waits for md, md for mdadm, for
ever.  A migration in place gets there: the AMD driver takes the
regions of a RAID0 in stripes of the new layout
(RC_TransformDynamicProtectSize), its log may stand across a stripe of
the old one (0x29c00 of a RAID0 of 3 given a fourth disk), and a region
may end across one past the suspend window (0x2580 of a RAID0 4 -> 5) -
going on with the AMD driver's own second migration of a RAID0 too.
manage_reshape_amd() ends a region that would take md past the window
where the window ends, a point where stripes of both layouts end, and
moves the window only there; the AMD driver works its regions out from
where it is.  At assembly recover_backup_amd() moves a migration stopped
across an old stripe on to such a point itself, before md runs, as it
moves the first stripes through AMD's log of the data.

Tested with the AMD Linux driver (rcraid 9.3.3) in a QEMU guest: mdadm
finishes the AMD driver's migration stopped midway (a RAID0 in either
phase of a stripe in the log of the data, a RAID5), rcraid finishes
ours, the final LDs those of rcraid byte for byte.  The hang, reproduced
on loop devices from the AMD driver's marks of a RAID0 (3 -> 4, 4 -> 5),
is gone with this.  Not yet tried on hardware or with the AMD Windows
driver.

Written by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI model by Anthropic.
